<Layout title="EPA and Varmebaronen – Is it the end of the line? | Smokeless Heat Blog" description="Varmebaronen was sent a letter from the EPA after they requested to renew their certificates for the Vedolux boilers.">
  <Header />

  <main class="pt-24">
    <section class="py-16 bg-charcoal text-cream">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6">
        <p class="text-ember text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.3em] mb-4">News</p>
        <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl">EPA and Varmebaronen – Is it the end of the line?</h1>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="py-16 bg-cream">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 space-y-6 text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed">
        <img src="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/epatear-1024x1024.jpg" alt="EPA letter" class="w-full border border-wood/20" />

        <p>
          Varmebaronen was sent a letter from the EPA after they requested to renew their certificates for the Vedolux boilers. The letter arrived before the certificates expired and gave Varmebaronen only 10 days to resolve various deficiencies the EPA found when reviewing the original test. The original tests had been completed roughly five years earlier. The test agency, Research Institutes of Sweden (RISE), became an approved EPA test site so it could test the boilers for Varmebaronen. The lab worked to the best of its ability conducting the tests because Method 28-PTS was being used for the first time under the new certification rules. The lab provided the information the EPA requested at that time. At the end of the day the EPA accepted the results and issued the certifications for each boiler that was tested.
        </p>

        <p>
          All of the items in the EPA deficiency letter could have been easily fixed at the time. However, with the passing of time it is no longer easy to produce the requested information. Items such as additional manual text explaining what can and cannot be burned, the importance of smoke-detector language, more details on draft, and how to exercise the warranty would have been straightforward to correct. Other information simply could not be reproduced without a new test. These items included close-up photos of the individual wood pieces used in the original test, a signature of the person who performed the conditioning burn of the test boilers and their qualifications, a revision of the report to show that fuel density exceeded 10 lb/ft³, inclusion of all notes taken by test personnel during the process, and any instructions the manufacturer may have given the test agency to keep the notes consistent with the manual, among other items. For the most part these items should not have affected the end result of the test; they are simply paperwork or technicalities that follow the “letter” of the law. A retest using the same protocol would produce similar results, so this is not a case of a dirty boiler slipping through by gaming the system.
        </p>

        <p>
          Varmebaronen did not plan on testing all of its models again so soon and was seeking a renewal of the certification because nothing had materially changed that would require a new test. The manufacturer and the test agency tried to work with the EPA to find a path forward, but given their experience dealing with the agency it felt as if the two sides were talking past each other and there was no clear way ahead. They were not confident conducting another test because of the difficulty of communicating with the EPA and the uncertainty of how the results might be interpreted. Different people can read the same NSPS text and reach more than one conclusion. It seemed to Varmebaronen that there was no clarity and that anything done now would likely be obsolete within five years. New rounds of testing would most likely be required again. Varmebaronen decided it had to cut its losses until there is clear and consistent guidance from the regulators. I do not know whether the company will return to the U.S. market with certified models if the test methods are eventually resolved. At this time the situation appears to be “up in the air” at the EPA.
        </p>

        <p>
          Varmebaronen continues to manufacture the lambda models we sold. For the foreseeable future we will continue to support owners with parts, including any legacy models. We still have a few units that were manufactured while the models held active certifications, and we will sell them until the stock is depleted. The remaining lambda models carry all of the proper EPA labeling.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you want to see the problems with the EPA wood-heater program and the uncertainty that is evident, please see the “Report at a Glance” and the full report here: <a href="https://www.epa.gov/office-inspector-general/report-epas-residential-wood-heater-program-does-not-provide-reasonable" class="text-ember hover:underline">Report: The EPA’s Residential Wood Heater Program Does Not Provide Reasonable Assurance that Heaters Are Properly Tested and Certified Before Reaching Consumers</a>.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-wood mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-wood/20">
          Originally published: June 2023
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
